      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's part 2 of 4 of an OIML certificate PDF for water meters (R49 recommendation). The certificate number is R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 7, project number 3528374, issued by NMi (Netherlands).\n\nThe fragment contains several tables of flow rates by meter size and accuracy class. These appear to be:\n1. A table (continuing from part 1 presumably) with DN40-DN150\n2. Page 5: table with DN200-DN600\n3. Page 6: Table 4 \"General characteristics of the family of instruments - Full bore - AC mains powered\" with DN40-DN900\n4. Page 7: continuation with DN1000-DN1800\n\nSo there are at least two different tables/series: the first tables (pages 4-5, spanning the fragment) appear to be one set (possibly \"Full bore - battery powered\" or similar from part 1), and the Table 4 on pages 6-7 is \"Full bore - AC mains powered\".\n\nSince this is a fragment, I need to extract:\n- certificate info: number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 7, project_number 3528374, page_total 12, member_state Netherlands, issuing authority NMi.\n\nWait — the fragment shows header info repeated per page: \"NMi\", \"OIML Certificate\", \"OIML Member State: The Netherlands\", Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 7, Project number 3528374, Page 5 of 12.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" with revision 7. The scheme is A (from \"-A-\" in the number). Page total = 12.\n\nFor the certificate object:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- revision: \"7\" — hmm, the schema has revision_history but not a top-level revision. The number includes \"revision 7\". I could include it in number or as revision_history. The schema doesn't have a revision field at certificate level. I might add revision to number as-is: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and maybe revision_history: [{revision: \"7\"}] — but no date/changes given. Better to keep number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and possibly include revision in it. Actually, let me include the number as given: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add revision_history with revision \"7\" and null date/changes? Rule 8 says don't fabricate. The revision is present (\"revision 7\"). I could include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"7\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}]. Hmm, that seems acceptable but maybe risky. Alternatively include in number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 7\". I think putting the number without revision and adding revision_history entry \"7\" is cleaner. Actually the schema says revision_history entries have revision, date, changes. I'll include {\"revision\": \"7\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}. Hmm, but \"Empty strings are not values — use null or omit.\" I'll include date and changes as null.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — the \"revision 7\" is part of the number string in the header. I'll include revision_history as above to be safe.\n\n- project_number: \"3528374\"\n- page_total: 12\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- issuing_authority: name \"NMi\" (Nederlands Meetinstituut). The fragment only shows \"NMi\". I'll use \"NMi\". oiml_issuer_id: not given in fragment — omit. Actually NMi's OIML issuer ID... don't fabricate. Omit.\n\nRecommendation: R49, edition 2013. The number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" indicates OIML R49:2013. The text references \"OIML R49-1:2013\". So recommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [1, 2].\n\nCategory: water meters → \"Water meter\" or \"Measuring system\"? R49 is water meters for closed conduit. The tables mention \"Meter size\". Category: \"Water meter\".\n\nNow the tables. Two logical tables:\n1. Table (pages 4-5) — first two tables in fragment, DN40–DN150 (page 4 presumably) and DN200–DN600 (page 5). This is one continuous table split across pages. It doesn't have the \"Table 4 ... Full bore - AC mains powered\" title in this fragment — the title likely appeared in part 1 (page 4). Since the fragment starts with the table without a preceding title, this could be Table 3 or similar (maybe \"Full bore - battery powered\"). I don't know its name. I'll name it something descriptive but note I can't know the title. The rows: DN40 (1&2), DN50 (1&2), DN65 (1; 2), DN80 (1;2), DN100 (1;2), DN125 (1;2), DN150 (1&2), then page 5: DN200 (1&2), DN250 (1;2), DN300 (1;2), DN350 (1;2), DN400 (1), DN400 (2), DN450 (1), DN450 (2), DN500 (1), DN500 (2), DN600 (1), DN600 (2).\n\n2. Table 4 \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore - AC mains powered\" (pages 6-7): DN40–DN900 (page 6) and DN1000–DN1800 (page 7).\n\nNow how to represent. The schema suggests matrix_tables for multi-dim tables AND populating model_family.models and characteristics.model_level.\n\nThe models here: each meter size × accuracy class combination defines a variant. The varying attributes: q1_min, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1.\n\nModels: model_id like \"DN40 class 1&2\"? Or just meter sizes with class axis? The tables have two dimensions: meter size and accuracy class. Model variants: e.g., \"DN65 class 1\", \"DN65 class 2\". Since each row is a unique combination, I'll define models per row.\n\nThat's a lot of models: table 1 has: DN40(1&2), DN50(1&2), DN65(1), DN65(2), DN80(1), DN80(2), DN100(1), DN100(2), DN125(1), DN125(2), DN150(1&2), DN200(1&2), DN250(1), DN250(2), DN300(1), DN300(2), DN350(1), DN350(2), DN400(1), DN400(2), DN450(1), DN450(2), DN500(1), DN500(2), DN600(1), DN600(2) = 26 rows.\n\nTable 4 has: DN40(1&2), DN50(1&2), DN65(1&2), DN80(1&2), DN100(1&2), DN125(1&2), DN150(1), DN150(2), DN200(1), DN200(2), DN250(1), DN250(2), DN300(1), DN300(2), DN350(1&2), DN400(1&2), DN450(1&2), DN500(1&2), DN600(1&2), DN700(1), DN700(2), DN750(1), DN750(2), DN800(1), DN800(2), DN900(1), DN900(2), DN1000(1), DN1000(2), DN1050(1), DN1050(2), DN1100(1), DN1100(2), DN1200(1), DN1200(2), DN1350(1), DN1350(2), DN1400(1), DN1400(2), DN1500(1), DN1500(2), DN1600(1), DN1600(2), DN1650(1), DN1650(2), DN1800(1&2) = 46 rows.\n\nThat's a lot of JSON. But the instruction says to preserve the tables and populate model_level. Given it's a fragment task, I should do it thoroughly. The output will be large but that's fine.\n\nLet me convert decimal commas: 0,0625 → 0.0625; 787,5 → 787.5; etc.\n\nTable 1 rows (flow rates m³/h):\n\nDN40, class 1&2: Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.1, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 400\nDN50, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\nDN65, 1: 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\nDN65, 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\nDN80, 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\nDN80, 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\nDN100, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\nDN100, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\nDN125, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\nDN125, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\nDN150, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\nDN200, 1&2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\nDN250, 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\nDN250, 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\nDN300, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\nDN300, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\nDN350, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\nDN350, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\nDN400, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\nDN400, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\nDN450, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\nDN450, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\nDN500, 1: 100, 160, 4000, 5000, 40\nDN500, 2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\nDN600, 1: 157.5, 252, 6300, 7875, 40\nDN600, 2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nTable 4 rows (Full bore - AC mains powered):\n\nDN40, 1&2: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\nDN50, 1&2: 0.126, 0.2016, 63, 78.75, 500\nDN65, 1&2: 0.2, 0.32, 100, 125, 500\nDN80, 1&2: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\nDN100, 1&2: 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\nDN125, 1&2: 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\nDN150, 1: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\nDN150, 2: 1.26, 2.016, 630, 787.5, 500\nDN200, 1: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\nDN200, 2: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\nDN250, 1: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\nDN250, 2: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\nDN300, 1: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\nDN300, 2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\nDN350, 1&2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\nDN400, 1&2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\nDN450, 1&2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\nDN500, 1&2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\nDN600, 1&2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\nDN700, 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\nDN700, 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\nDN750, 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\nDN750, 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\nDN800, 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 200?? no wait: DN800, 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200. DN800, 1: 100,160,10000,12500,100.\nDN900, 1: 158.7, 253.9, 10000, 12500, 63\nDN900, 2: 80, 128, 10000, 12500, 125\nDN1000, 1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\nDN1000, 2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\nDN1050, 1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\nDN1050, 2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\nDN1100, 1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\nDN1100, 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\nDN1200, 1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\nDN1200, 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\nDN1350, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\nDN1350, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\nDN1400, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\nDN1400, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\nDN1500, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\nDN1500, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\nDN1600, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\nDN1600, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\nDN1650, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\nDN1650, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\nDN1800, 1&2: 317.5, 507.9, 16000, 20000, 50\n\nNow, how to structure. Two matrix_tables entries:\n\n1. name: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments\" — but wait, we don't know the title of the first table since it's on page 4 (part 1). The fragment starts with a table then the page 5 header. Actually the first table appears BEFORE the \"Page 5\" header, so it's from page 4 (part 1). Since this is a fragment of a bigger cert, the first table's title isn't in this fragment. I'll describe it as \"General characteristics (meter size / accuracy class) — continuation\" or maybe just \"General characteristics of the family of instruments (part)\". Hmm.\n\nGiven the cert family, likely page 4 table is \"Table 3 General characteristics of the family of instruments - Full bore - battery powered\" or \"part 1\". I shouldn't fabricate. I'll name it \"Meter size / accuracy class flow rates (continued from previous page)\" — but that's awkward. Let me just name it descriptively: \"Flow rates by meter size and accuracy class\" and Table 4 as \"Table 4 General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore - AC mains powered\".\n\nAlso note: Q1 and Q2 are minimum values; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values (per the note). I could add a note in description/footnotes.\n\nNow model_level characteristics. Should I create one set of model_level for table 1's models and one for table 4's? The model_level values have \"model\" keys. If both tables share models like \"DN40 class 1&2\"... Table 1 DN40 1&2: Q3=25; Table 4 DN40 1&2: Q3=40. These are different configurations (battery powered vs AC mains powered — actually the difference is likely battery vs AC powered variants). So they're distinct variants.\n\nHmm, so model_family.models would need to distinguish: e.g., \"DN40 class 1&2 (AC mains)\" vs \"DN40 class 1&2\". Since we know Table 4 is AC mains powered, and Table 1 is presumably the battery-powered variant (title not in fragment), I'll label table 4 models with \"AC mains\" suffix? That could be fabricating for table 1. Alternative: name models like \"DN65 class 1\" for table 1 and \"DN65 class 1 (AC mains powered)\" for table 4. Since Table 4's title explicitly says \"Full bore - AC mains powered\", that's grounded. For table 1, the title is absent; I can leave the models plain and note in matrix_tables description that title is on a previous page.\n\nActually, to keep model_level consistent and avoid ambiguity, I could key models as \"DN40 class 1&2\" for the first table and \"DN40 class 1&2 (AC mains)\" for table 4. And the model_family.models list includes all of them with attributes noting family/power supply.\n\nGiven size constraints, maybe a cleaner approach: put both tables in matrix_tables with full rows (this preserves all data), and populate model_level characteristics for each table. But model_level is a single array — I can add entries with attribute names distinguished, e.g., attribute \"q1_min\" with values referencing models that include the config in the model id. That works if model ids are unique across the whole cert.\n\nSo:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null (no explicit family name given; maybe \"family of instruments\" — Table 4 says \"General characteristics of the family of instruments\". I could set family_name null.)\n- models: list of all model ids.\n\nHmm, that's 26 + 46 = 72 models. That's a huge JSON. Is that necessary? The schema example for R137 populated model_family.models with just model ids. Given the axis structure (size × class), maybe models should be per meter size with attributes {accuracy_classes: [1,2]} and config-level? Hmm.\n\nAlternative interpretation: The model variants are meter sizes (DN40...DN1800), and accuracy class is a configuration axis within each size. Then characteristics.config_level could handle class-dependent values: attribute q1_min, axis \"accuracy_class\", values [{condition: \"DN65 class 1\", value: 0.39375}...]. That's basically the same size.\n\nI think the cleanest: treat each table row (size × class) as a model variant. model_id like \"DN65-1\". Then model_level entries per attribute.\n\nBut wait — model_level values shape: {\"model\", \"value\", \"footnote_markers\"} — no unit per value; unit at attribute level. Good.\n\nGiven the size, I'll produce:\n\nmatrix_tables: two entries, each with columns [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1_min\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"], column_units [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null], rows = the rows.\n\nmodel_level: for each table, four attributes × values. That's 8 attribute entries with 26 and 46 values each. Total values: 26*5*... wait per table attributes: q1_min, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 → 5 attributes. Two tables → 10 attribute entries. Values count: (26+46)*5 = 360 value objects. That's big but acceptable.\n\nHmm, but is it worth it? The instructions emphasize matrix detection as MOST IMPORTANT, and to extract both matrix_tables AND populate model_level. I'll do it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe I should reduce duplication by making model_level only for one table and rely on matrix_tables for both? The example shows both populated from one table. With two tables, I'll populate both into model_level with distinct model ids.\n\nModel ID naming: \"DN40 class 1&2\" contains \"&\". Fine in JSON. Let me use format like \"DN65 class 1\". For AC mains table: \"DN65 class 1 (AC mains)\". Hmm, or since the first table's power supply is unknown in this fragment, maybe the two tables are actually different series: e.g., page 4-5 table could be \"reduced bore\"? Unknown. I'll just suffix the second table's models with \"(AC mains powered)\" and leave the first table's models without suffix, describing in the matrix_tables entry that it's a continuation whose title precedes this fragment.\n\nActually wait — maybe better to keep model ids identical style: \"DN40 class 1&2\" would collide between tables. To disambiguate, for table 4 (AC mains), model ids get \" (AC mains powered)\" suffix. Table 1 models plain. I'll also add an attribute in models entries: e.g., {\"model_id\": \"DN65 class 1 (AC mains powered)\", \"attributes\": {\"meter_size\": \"DN65\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}}. For table 1 models, attributes {\"meter_size\": \"DN65\", \"accuracy_class\": 1}.\n\nHmm, for class \"1 & 2\" accuracy class is both — value \"1 & 2\" or [1,2]? I'll use \"1 & 2\" as string or \"1&2\". Let me use \"1 & 2\".\n\nLet me reconsider whether to include model_family at all. Rule: \"model_family: include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\". Yes it does. OK.\n\nNow certificate-level:\n\n```json\n\"certificate\": {\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"3528374\",\n  \"page_total\": 12,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"date_issued\": null\n}\n```\ndate_issued not in fragment — omit. oiml_issuer_id not present — omit.\n\nRevision: \"revision 7\" appears with the number. I'll add revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"7\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}]. Hmm, date/changes null... rule says omit if not present. The revision_history schema requires revision, date, changes fields; I'll include with nulls. Actually \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" — I'll omit date and changes keys entirely, leaving just revision. But schema shows all three keys... I think including {\"revision\": \"7\"} alone is acceptable. Hmm, alternatively include \"revision\" inside number string. Let me think about which is better for downstream parsing. The certificate number field: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". The revision is metadata. I'll do revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"7\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}].\n\nActually, I worry the grader might see nulls as fabricated values. Null means \"not present\" though. Fine.\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\". address_lines not present — omit. person fields — omit. \n\nHmm, should I include issuing_authority at all with just name? Yes.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [1, 2].\n\ncertified_type: category \"Water meter\", type_designations — not given in fragment (probably in part 1). Omit type_designations? The models are DN sizes... those are meter sizes, not type designations. The cert is for a family of instruments. I'll omit type_designations since not present in this fragment. Or should model ids go there? No — model_family covers it. description: not present; omit. module_designation: not applicable/omit.\n\nActually wait — certified_type category: The tables are for water meters (R49 = Water meters for closed conduits... actually R49-1:2013 is \"Water meters for cold potable water and hot water\"). Category: \"Water meter\". This is inference from R49 which is well-grounded (recommen