Q3 = 1.25); Q3/Q1 ratio at least 40; Q3 and Q3/Q1 selected from OIML R49-1:2013 §4.1.\"\n\nDecimal conversion note: \"Q2/Q1 = 1,6\" → 1.6.\n\nNow model_family. Should I include it? With 72 models it's huge. Alternatively, since the two axes are size and class, maybe models per meter size with attributes including accuracy classes... But flow rates vary by class, so model per size-class row is the cleanest. However the sheer size — the answer will be long but that's OK.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Maybe a leaner approach: model_family.models lists unique meter sizes as models (48 sizes? Let's count unique sizes: table1: DN40,50,65,80,100,125,150,200,250,300,350,400,450,500,600 = 15 sizes; table4: DN40,50,65,80,100,125,150,200,250,300,350,400,450,500,600,700,750,800,900,1000,1050,1100,1200,1350,1400,1500,1600,1650,1800 = 29 sizes. Union = 29 unique sizes since table1 ⊂ table4 sizes). And accuracy_class as a config axis in config_level. But then config_level values {condition: \"DN65 class 1\", value: 0.39375} — condition would be \"model=DN65, class=1\". This is essentially equivalent complexity.\n\nI think per-row model ids is cleanest and most faithful. Let me commit to model ids per row.\n\nTotal models: 72. In model_family.models, each entry {\"model_id\": \"DN65 class 1\", \"attributes\": {\"meter_size\": \"DN65\", \"accuracy_class\": \"1\", \"power_supply\": null}}? Don't include null power_supply; for table-4 models add \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\" and \"bore\": \"full bore\". For table-1 models, power supply unknown → omit.\n\nWait — actually, is Table 4's \"Full bore - AC mains powered\" heading implying there's a companion table for battery powered elsewhere (probably Table 3 on page 4, which is the first table in this fragment)? Yes, very likely. But I can't confirm from fragment. I'll just note in matrix_tables[0].description: \"Table title precedes this fragment (page 4); continuation on page 5.\"\n\nAlternatively, I might label table-1 models as e.g. \"DN65 class 1\" and table-4 as \"DN65 class 1 (AC mains powered)\". Good.\n\nmodel_family.family_name: null? Or omit family_name. The schema shows family_name as a field; I'll set null since no explicit name given (\"family of instruments\" is generic — could use that? I'll use null).\n\nNow let me think about whether to split model_level attributes per table or combine. Since model ids are unique per table (suffix), one combined array works. I'll have 10 attribute entries: 5 for table-1 models, 5 for table-4 models. But attributes have same names (\"minimum_flow_q1\" twice) with different model sets. That's fine — the attribute repeats with disjoint model values. Hmm, might be confusing. Alternatively, prefix attribute names... no, attribute should stay canonical. The model id disambiguates. OK.\n\nActually, maybe cleaner: attributes identical names, and each entry's values list only contains models from one table. Two entries with attribute \"minimum_flow_q1\" — one for regular models, one for AC mains models. Acceptable.\n\nLet me now also double check decimal conversions:\n- 0,0625 → 0.0625 ✓\n- 0,1 → 0.1 ✓\n- 31,25 → 31.25\n- 0,39375 → 0.39375\n- 0,1575 → 0.1575\n- 0,252 → 0.252\n- 1,575 → 1.575\n- 2,52 → 2.52\n- 787,5 → 787.5\n- 6,25 → 6.25\n- 100,8 → 100.8\n- 15,625 → 15.625\n- 0,08 → 0.08\n- 0,126 → 0.126\n- 0,2016 → 0.2016\n- 0,32 → 0.32\n- 0,512 → 0.512\n- 4,032 → 4.032\n- 1,26 → 1.26\n- 2,016 → 2.016\n- 10,24 → 10.24\n- 5,12 → 5.12\n- 158,7 → 158.7\n- 253,9 → 253.9\n- 406,2 → 406.2\n- 204,8 → 204.8\n- 317,5 → 317.5\n- 507,9 → 507.9\n\nGood.\n\nNow build JSON.\n\nStructure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3528374\",\n    \"page_total\": 12,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"mod